<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/1st-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Munda</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/45-bce">45 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caesars-civil-war">Caesar's Civil War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Munda was fought on 17 March 45 BCE in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> and the Pompeian army of Gnaeus and Sextus Pompeius and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/titus-labienus">Titus Labienus</a>. After a hard fight Caesar won; Labienus was killed, and Gnaeus Pompeius was caught and executed soon afterwards. It was Caesar's last battle of the civil war.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-munda.jpg" alt="Battle of Munda" width="320" height="148" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Munda_(M._Merian).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The republican cause had been led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a> until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-pharsalus">Battle of Pharsalus</a> in 48 BCE, and Caesar destroyed their army at Thapsus in April 46 BCE. Resistance then centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a>. In the spring of 46 BCE two legions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania-ulterior">Hispania Ulterior</a>, largely former Pompeian veterans, declared for Gnaeus Pompeius and drove out Caesar's proconsul. They were joined by the brothers Gnaeus and Sextus Pompeius, Publius Attius Varus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/titus-labienus">Titus Labienus</a>, a trusted general of Caesar in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallic-wars">Gallic Wars</a>, and raised four legions. Caesar travelled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> to Obulco in less than a month, arriving in early December.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The campaign took Caesar past Ulia, Corduba and Ategua, which he besieged through the winter and which surrendered in early 45 BCE, damaging Pompeian morale. After a skirmish near Soricaria on 7 March, many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> in the Pompeian camp began planning to defect, and Gnaeus Pompeius gave up his delaying tactics and marched south towards Munda.</p><p>On 17 March 45 BCE Caesar, with eight legions and 8,000 horsemen, faced thirteen legions, 6,000 light infantry and about 6,000 horsemen on a hill less than a mile from the walls of Munda. Many Pompeian soldiers had surrendered before and rejoined, and fought desperately. The fighting lasted eight hours, and Caesar later said that at Munda he had fought for his life. His Tenth Legion began to push back the Pompeians. Gnaeus Pompeius weakened his right wing to reinforce his left, and King Bogud's Mauretanian cavalry broke through. The Pompeians took Labienus's move for retreat and fled.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>About 30,000 Pompeians were reported dead on the field, while Caesar claimed to have lost only 1,000, though his real losses were probably higher. Labienus and Attius Varus died and Caesar had them buried. Gnaeus Pompeius escaped the field and was later cornered and killed. Corduba surrendered and paid a heavy indemnity, and Munda yielded with 14,000 prisoners. Sextus Pompeius remained at large, but no conservative army challenged Caesar again. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> says his triumph for the victory displeased the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>, as it marked the destruction of the family of one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>'s great men. He was murdered on 15 March the next year. The site of Munda remains disputed, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ronda">Ronda</a>, Montilla and the area near Osuna proposed.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Munda” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the civil wars it was a stronghold of the Pompeians, defeated by Caesar at <b>Munda</b> in 45 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/hispania-ulterior">Hispania Ulterior</a></li></ul></section>
